#30 positive.cpp:215:1-24 - cpp-macro-constant: prefer a `constexpr` variable instead of a macro to define a constant, as it has a type and a scope (`#define BUFFER_SIZE 512` -> `constexpr auto BUFFER_SIZE = 512;`).
#31 positive.cpp:216:1-25 - cpp-macro-constant: prefer a `constexpr` variable instead of a macro to define a constant, as it has a type and a scope (`#define GREETING "hello"` -> `constexpr auto GREETING = "hello";`).
#32 positive.cpp:217:1-31 - cpp-typedef: prefer a `using` alias instead of `typedef`, as it reads left to right and also works for templates (`typedef unsigned long Counter;` -> `using Counter = unsigned long;`).
#33 positive.cpp:218:1-31 - cpp-typedef: prefer a `using` alias instead of `typedef`, as it reads left to right and also works for templates (`typedef` -> `using`).
#34 positive.cpp:223:5-29 - c-insecure-function: prefer a bounded alternative to this function, as it writes past the end of the buffer when the input is longer than expected (`strcpy` -> `snprintf`).
#35 positive.cpp:224:5-32 - c-insecure-function: prefer a bounded alternative to this function, as it writes past the end of the buffer when the input is longer than expected (`vsprintf` -> `vsnprintf`).
#36 positive.cpp:225:13-22 - c-atoi: prefer strtol or strtod instead, as atoi cannot report invalid input or overflow (`atoi` -> `std::stoi`).
#37 positive.cpp:226:18-35 - cpp-c-style-cast: prefer a named cast instead of a C-style cast, as it states the conversion and the compiler rejects the unsafe ones (`(char *)malloc(n)` -> `static_cast<char *>(malloc(n))`, or `reinterpret_cast` between unrelated types).
#38 positive.cpp:226:26-35 - cpp-manual-memory: prefer containers and smart pointers instead of managing memory by hand, as a missed or doubled release leaks or corrupts memory (`malloc` -> `std::vector` or `std::make_unique`).
#39 positive.cpp:227:5-15 - cpp-manual-memory: prefer containers and smart pointers instead of managing memory by hand, as a missed or doubled release leaks or corrupts memory (`free` -> an owning container).
#40 positive.cpp:229:5-13 - cpp-manual-memory: prefer containers and smart pointers instead of managing memory by hand, as a missed or doubled release leaks or corrupts memory (`delete[]` -> a smart pointer or container).
#41 positive.cpp:230:20-29 - cpp-c-style-cast: prefer a named cast instead of a C-style cast, as it states the conversion and the compiler rejects the unsafe ones (`(double)n` -> `static_cast<double>(n)`).
#42 positive.cpp:231:25-39 - cpp-c-style-cast: prefer a named cast instead of a C-style cast, as it states the conversion and the compiler rejects the unsafe ones (`(std::size_t)n` -> `static_cast<std::size_t>(n)`).
#43 positive.cpp:232:9-26 - cpp-size-empty: prefer using `empty()` instead of comparing `size()` with zero, as it states the intent and is constant time for every container (`items.size() == 0` -> `items.empty()`).
#44 positive.cpp:233:52-61 - cpp-std-endl: prefer writing `'\n'` instead of `std::endl`, as `std::endl` also flushes the stream on every line (`std::endl` -> `'\n'`).
#45 positive.cpp:235:9-26 - cpp-size-empty: prefer using `empty()` instead of comparing `size()` with zero, as it states the intent and is constant time for every container (`0 != items.size()` -> `!items.empty()`).
#46 positive.cpp:236:9-43 - cpp-make-unique: prefer `std::make_unique` or `std::make_shared` instead of passing `new` to a smart pointer, as it is shorter and cannot leak when another argument throws (`w->child.reset(new Widget(n, src))` -> `w->child = std::make_unique<Widget>(n, src)` (`std::make_shared` for a shared_ptr)).
#47 positive.cpp:238:18-56 - cpp-make-unique: prefer `std::make_unique` or `std::make_shared` instead of passing `new` to a smart pointer, as it is shorter and cannot leak when another argument throws (`std::unique_ptr<Widget>(new Widget(n))` -> `std::make_unique<Widget>(n)`).
#48 positive.cpp:239:5-49 - cpp-make-unique: prefer `std::make_unique` or `std::make_shared` instead of passing `new` to a smart pointer, as it is shorter and cannot leak when another argument throws (`std::shared_ptr<Widget> second(new Widget())` -> `auto second = std::make_shared<Widget>()`).
#49 positive.cpp:240:14-18 - cpp-nullptr: prefer using `nullptr` instead of `NULL`, as `NULL` is an integer constant that can select the wrong overload (`NULL` -> `nullptr`).
#50 positive.cpp:249:21-25 - cpp-std-endl: prefer writing `'\n'` instead of `std::endl`, as `std::endl` also flushes the stream on every line (`endl` -> `'\n'`).
#51 positive.cpp:254:32-48 - cpp-size-empty: prefer using `empty()` instead of comparing `size()` with zero, as it states the intent and is constant time for every container (`rows.size() == 0` -> `rows.empty()`).
#52 positive.cpp:259:12-32 - no-formatted-string-placeholders: formatted string without any placeholders (`std::format("plain")` -> `std::string("plain")`).
#53 positive.cpp:264:8-28 - reviewer-addressed-comment: comment speaks to the reviewer or the person who asked for the change (`you may want to tune`).
#54 positive.cpp:266:9-51 - generic-error-message: error raised with a generic message that names neither what failed nor the value (`throw std::runtime_error("internal error")`).
#55 positive.cpp:268:5-22 - tautological-assertion: assertion compares literals or asserts a literal true, so it cannot fail (`EXPECT_TRUE(true)`).
#56 positive.cpp:269:5-20 - tautological-assertion: assertion compares literals or asserts a literal true, so it cannot fail (`ASSERT_EQ(4, 4)`).
#57 positive.hpp:4:1-21 - cpp-using-namespace-in-header: avoid `using namespace` at namespace scope in a header, as it leaks the names into every file that includes the header (`using namespace std;` -> qualified names).
